CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on the Establishment of the Korea Student Aid Foundation and matters necessary for enforcing that Act.

Article 2 (Scope of financial companies) "Institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as banks defined in Article 2(1)2 of the Banking Act" in subparagraph 7 of Article 2 of the Act on the Establishment of the Korea Student Aid Foundation (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means the following institutions: <Amended on Aug. 4, 2020; Jun. 22, 2021>
  1. Banks defined in Article 2(1)2 of the Banking Act;
  2. Financial companies, etc. defined in subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Act on Real Name Financial Transactions and Confidentiality, other than banks under subparagraph 1;
  3. Comprehensive credit information collection agencies under Article 25(2)1 of the Credit Information Use and Protection Act;
  4. Financial investment business entities under Article 8(1) of the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 7, 2011]

Article 2-2 (Eligible persons for conversion loans) Pursuant to Article 3-2 of the Act, persons eligible for a conversion loan shall be those who received a general repayment student loan or a credit guarantee between July 1, 2009 and December 31, 2012.
  [This Article Added on Jul. 5, 2022]

CHAPTER II KOREA STUDENT AID FOUNDATION

Article 3 (Registration of agents) (1) Where the President of the Korea Student Aid Foundation under Article 6 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Foundation") appoints an agent pursuant to Article 10-2 of the Act, the Foundation shall register the following matters at the location of its principal office within 2 weeks after such appointment; the same shall also apply where any registered matter is changed: <Amended on Jan. 21, 2025>
  1. The agent's name, resident registration number, and address;
  2. The name or location of the principal office, branch office, or local office for which the agent is appointed;
  3. Where the authority of the agent is restricted, the details of such restriction.
  (2) When filing a registration concerning the appointment of an agent, documents relating to the appointment of the agent shall be attached, and when filing a registration under paragraph (1)3, a document stating the details of the restriction shall be attached.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 7, 2011]
  [Previous Article 3 moved to Article 3-2 <Jun. 7, 2011>]

Article 3-2 (Agreements with entrusting entities of tuition fee aid programs) Pursuant to Article 16(1)5 of the Act, a person who entrusts a tuition fee aid program to the Foundation may enter into an agreement with the Foundation regarding the beneficiaries, amount of aid, eligibility criteria, methods of providing aid, and other matters related to such tuition fee aid program. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>
  [Moved from Article 3 <Jun. 7, 2011>]

Article 4 (Contribution grants) (1) Where the Foundation intends to receive a contribution pursuant to Article 17(1) of the Act, it shall submit to the Minister of Education, by April 30 of each year, a request for a contribution for the following year, accompanied by the following documents: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  1. A project plan under Article 16;
  2. An estimated income statement and estimated balance sheet;
  3. Other materials explaining the grounds for requesting the contribution.
  (2) Upon receipt of a request for a contribution under paragraph (1), the Minister of Education shall review its contents and, where he or she deems it necessary to provide the contribution, shall include the contribution requested by the Foundation in the budget. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) Where the government's provision of a contribution has been finalized, the Minister of Education shall notify the Foundation thereof. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (4) Upon receiving a notice under paragraph (3), the Foundation shall submit to the Minister of Education an application for the grant of the contribution, accompanied by a quarterly project plan and quarterly budget execution plan. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (5) The Foundation shall use contributions received from the government only for the purposes specified in the quarterly project plan and quarterly budget execution plan. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>

Article 5 (Application for bond issuance) To obtain approval for the issuance of bonds under Article 18 of the Act, the Foundation shall submit to the Minister of Education an application for bond issuance stating the amount of issuance, method of issuance, terms and conditions of issuance, and the method and procedures for redemption, and other necessary matters. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 6 (Form of bonds) Bonds shall be issued in bearer form; provided, upon request of a subscriber or holder, they may be issued in registered form.

Article 7 (Issuance at a discount) The Foundation may issue bonds at a discount.

Article 8 (Subscription to bonds) (1) Where the Foundation intends to issue bonds by public offering, it shall prepare and issue a bond subscription form containing the following matters: <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>
  1. The name of the Foundation;
  2. The total amount of bonds to be issued;
  3. The face value of each denomination of bonds;
  4. The interest rate of the bonds;
  5. The method and timing of principal redemption and interest payment;
  6. The issue price of the bonds or the minimum issue price thereof;
  7. Where there are any previously issued bonds that have not been redeemed, the total amount of such outstanding bonds;
  8. Where there is a financial company, etc. entrusted with the public offering of bonds pursuant to Article 36(1)4, its trade name and address.
  (2) A person intending to subscribe to a bond offering shall submit to the Foundation 2 copies of a bond subscription form stating the number of bonds to be underwritten, the underwriting price, and the subscriber's address, bearing the subscriber's name and seal. In such cases, where bonds are issued with a minimum issue price fixed, the subscription price shall be stated.

Article 9 (Method of full underwriting) ‘ Article 8 shall not apply where the total amount of bonds is underwritten pursuant to a contract. The same shall also apply to the portion underwritten by a financial company, etc. entrusted with the public offering of bonds. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>

Article 10 (Total amount of bond issuance) When issuing bonds, the Foundation may indicate in the bond subscription form that the bonds will be issued even where the total amount actually subscribed falls short of the total amount of bonds to be issued stated in the bond subscription form. In such cases, the total amount subscribed shall be deemed the total amount of bonds issued.

Article 11 (Payment of bond underwriting amounts) (1) Upon completion of the bond subscription process, the Foundation shall, without delay, require subscribers to pay in full the amount of the bonds underwritten by them.
  (2) A financial company, etc. entrusted with the public offering of bonds may perform the acts under paragraph (1) on behalf of the Foundation in its own name. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>
  (3) Where bonds are issued by public offering, the bonds shall not be issued until the full amount of payments corresponding to the total amount of bonds to be issued has been paid in.

Article 12 (Matters to be stated on bonds) The following matters shall be stated on bonds, and the President of the Foundation shall affix his or her name and seal thereto:
  1. The matters specified in Article 8(1)1 through 5;
  2. The serial number of the bond;
  3. The date of issuance of the bond.

Article 13 (Bond register) (1) The Foundation shall keep a bond register at its principal office and enter the following matters therein:
  1. The number and serial numbers of bonds by denomination;
  2. The date of issuance of the bonds;
  3. The matters specified in Article 8(1)2 through 5 and 8.
  (2) Where bonds are issued in registered form, the Foundation shall additionally enter the following matters, in addition to those specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1):
  1. The name and address of the bondholder;
  2. The date of acquisition of the bonds.
  (3) A bondholder or bearer may request inspection of the bond register.

Article 14 (Notices to bond subscribers and holders) (1) Any notice or demand to a subscriber to bonds or a person entitled thereto before the issuance of the bonds shall be sent to the address stated in the bond subscription form; provided, where the Foundation has been separately notified of another address, such notice or demand shall be sent to that address.
  (2) Any notice or demand to the bearer of bearer bonds shall be made by public notice; provided, where the address of the bearer is known, such notice or demand may be sent to that address.
  (3) Any notice or demand to the holder of registered bonds shall be sent to the address entered in the bond register. In such cases, where the Foundation has been separately notified of another address, such notice or demand shall be sent to that address.

Article 15 (Gratis loan of State or public property) (1) The conditions and procedures for leasing State or public property without charge or permitting its use and enjoyment without charge pursuant to Article 19(1) of the Act shall be determined by a contract entered into between the Foundation and the head of the relevant central government agency or the head of the relevant local government having jurisdiction over such State or public property. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>
  (2) Where the Foundation uses State or public property leased to it without charge or made available for its use and enjoyment without charge pursuant to Article 19(1) of the Act for any purpose other than its intended purpose, the head of the relevant central government agency or the head of the relevant local government having jurisdiction over such property may terminate the contract. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>
  (3) Except as otherwise provided in the Act or this Decree, the relevant provisions of the State Property Act and the Public Property and Commodity Management Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the gratis lease of, or gratis use and enjoyment of, State or public property.

Article 16 (Submission of project plans) (1) Pursuant to Article 21 of the Act, the Foundation shall submit a project plan and budget for the following year to the Minister of Education before the commencement of the relevant project year. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (2) A project plan under paragraph (1) shall include the following matters:
  1. The objectives of the project;
  2. The implementing entity and beneficiaries of the project;
  3. Details of the project;
  4. Project costs;
  5. Other matters necessary for carrying out the projects of the Foundation.

Article 17 (Submission of settlement of accounts) The Foundation shall attach the following documents to the statement of revenues and expenditures under Article 22 of the Act:
  1. The balance sheet and income statement for each project year;
  2. A comparative statement of the project plan for each project year and its implementation results;
  3. An audit opinion issued by a certified public accountant or an accounting firm;
  4. The minutes of the board of directors meeting at which the settlement of revenues and expenditures was approved.

CHAPTER III Deleted.

Article 18 Deleted. <Jun. 7, 2011>

Article 19 Deleted. <Jun. 7, 2011>

Article 20 Deleted. <Jun. 7, 2011>

Article 21 (Management of surplus funds) (1) "Methods prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 3 of Article 23-2 of the Act means the following methods: <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>
  1. Purchasing bonds the payment of which is guaranteed by a financial company, etc.;
  2. Underwriting or purchasing stocks, corporate bonds, and other securities.
  (2) The subjects and scope of the underwriting or purchase of stocks, corporate bonds, and other securities under paragraph (1)2 shall be determined by the Minister of Education. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 21-2 (Use of the loan account) (1) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 24-4(1)6 of the Act means any of the following matters: <Amended on Sep. 30, 2014>
  1. Research and surveys for improving the student loan system;
  2. Promotion of the student loan project.
  (2) "Persons prescribed by Presidential Decree, including recipients of livelihood benefits or medical benefits under Article 7(1)1 or 3 of the National Basic Living Security Act" in Article 24-4(2) of the Act means a university student who has received a student loan (including a university student who has received a student loan guaranteed by the Student Loan Credit Guarantee Account) and who falls under any of the following categories: <Added on Sep. 30, 2014; Aug. 29, 2016>
  1. A recipient of livelihood benefits under Article 7(1)1 of the National Basic Living Security Act, or his or her child;
  1-2. A recipient of medical benefits under Article 7(1)3 of the National Basic Living Security Act, or his or her child;
  1-3. A recipient of housing benefits under Article 7(1)2 of the National Basic Living Security Act who falls under any of the following items, or his or her child:
  a. A person participating in projects necessary for self-support under Article 9(5) of the National Basic Living Security Act;
  b. A person whose out-of-pocket expenses have been reduced pursuant to subparagraph 3d of Appendix 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the National Health Insurance Act;
  1-4. A recipient of education benefits under Article 7(1)4 of the National Basic Living Security Act who falls under any of the following items, or a member of his or her household:
  a. A person participating in projects necessary for self-support under Article 9(5) of the National Basic Living Security Act;
  b. A person whose out-of-pocket expenses have been reduced pursuant to subparagraph 3d of Appendix 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the National Health Insurance Act;
  2. A person belonging to the near-poverty class defined in subparagraph 10 of Article 2 of the National Basic Living Security Act who falls under any of the following items, or his or her child:
  a. A person participating in projects necessary for self-support under Article 9(5) of the National Basic Living Security Act;
  b. A person whose out-of-pocket expenses have been reduced pursuant to subparagraph 3d of Appendix 2 of the Enforcement Decree of the National Health Insurance Act;
  3. A person eligible for support under Article 5 or 5-2 of the Single-Parent Family Support Act;
  4. A person receiving a disability allowance under Article 49 of the Welfare of Persons with Disabilities Act, or a person receiving a disability child allowance under Article 50(1) of that Act;
  5. A person receiving a disability pension pursuant to Article 4 of the Act on the Guarantee of Income for Persons with Disabilities;
  6. A person meeting the criteria determined by the Minister of Education in consideration of the university student's academic performance and household income level.
  (3) Detailed matters concerning interest support for persons eligible under paragraph (2) shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Education. <Added on Sep. 30, 2014>
  [This Article Added on Jun. 7, 2011]

Article 21-3 (Extension and exemption of repayment) (1) "Grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 24-10(1) of the Act means any of the following cases where a person has received a general repayment student loan: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 30, 2014; Jun. 30, 2020>
  1. Where eligibility to receive job-seeking benefits has been recognized pursuant to Article 43 of the Employment Insurance Act;
  2. Where the person serves in any of the following capacities pursuant to the Military Service Act:
  a. An active-duty soldier under Article 16 of the Military Service Act;
  b. A member of the full-time reserve service under Article 21 of the Military Service Act;
  c. A social service agent under Article 26 of the Military Service Act;
  d. An alternative service worker under Article 17 of the Act on the Assignment and Service of Alternative Military Service Personnel;
  3. Where the person advances to a higher-level school or studies abroad;
  4. Where the Minister of Education otherwise deems it necessary to extend the repayment period of a student loan.
  (2) To obtain an exemption from repayment of all or part of a student loan pursuant to Article 24-10(2) of the Act, any of the following persons shall apply to the Foundation for exemption from repayment of all or part of a general repayment student loan by submitting an application for exemption from repayment of a general repayment student loan together with relevant documents: <Amended on Jul. 19, 2016; Apr. 14, 2020>
  1. The person concerned or his or her agent;
  2. The spouse, a lineal blood relative, or a collateral blood relative within the 4th degree of kinship.
  (3) The relevant documents referred to in the part other than the subparagraphs of paragraph (2) shall be as follows: <Amended on Jul. 19, 2016; Apr. 14, 2020>
  1. A family relations certificate under Article 15(1)1 of the Act on the Registration of Family Relationships (attached only where the application is filed by a person falling under paragraph (2)2);
  1-2. A power of attorney or other document proving the agency relationship (attached only where an agent files the application under paragraph (2)1);
  2. A death certificate or other document proving death (attached only where the person concerned is deceased);
  3. A disability registration card under Article 32(1) of the Welfare of Persons with Disabilities Act, a document confirming that the person is a beneficiary under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Act on the Guarantee of Income for Persons with Disabilities, or any other document confirming the degree of the person's physical or mental disability (attached only in cases other than the death of the person concerned);
  4. Where an heir of the person concerned has obtained qualified acceptance of inheritance, a document proving that fact (attached only where the person concerned is deceased).
  (4) Upon receiving an application for exemption under paragraph (2), the Foundation may exempt all or part of the repayment of a student loan in consideration of the degree of physical or mental disability, income, assets, and other relevant circumstances of the person who received the general repayment student loan. In such cases, detailed matters concerning the standards for exemption from repayment shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Education. <Added on Apr. 14, 2020>
  [This Article Added on Jun. 7, 2011]

Article 21-4 (Timing for establishing plans for the student loan project) The Minister of Education shall establish the plan under Article 24-11(2) of the Act by the end of October every 3rd year commencing in 2010. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on Jun. 7, 2011]

Article 21-5 (Establishment of loan limits by higher education institution) (1) The criteria for establishing loan limits by higher education institution under Article 24-11(2) of the Act shall be determined by the Minister of Education, taking into consideration the quality of higher education and the financial soundness of the loan system, following deliberation by the Student Aid System Deliberation Committee under Article 49-3 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee"). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 30, 2014>
  (2) The Minister of Education may, in accordance with the criteria for establishing loan limits by higher education institution under paragraph (1), select universities to which loan limits will be applied each year. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The Minister of Education may disclose the criteria for establishing loan limits by higher education institution under paragraph (1) and the results of the selection under paragraph (2). <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  [This Article Added on Jun. 7, 2011]

Article 22 (Borrowing of funds) (1) "Funds prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 26(1)6 of the Act means any of the following borrowings made at the expense of the Student Loan Credit Guarantee Account under Article 25 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Guarantee Account") where funds are temporarily insufficient for the operation of the Guarantee Account: <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>
  1. Borrowings from government accounts or the Bank of Korea;
  2. Borrowings from financial companies, etc.
  (2) The principal and interest of borrowings under paragraph (1) shall be repaid within the relevant fiscal year.

Article 23 (Contributions to the guarantee account by financial companies) "Ratio prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 26(3) of the Act means 3/1,000. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>
  [Title Amended on Jun. 7, 2011]

Article 24 (Use of the Guarantee Account) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 5 of Article 27 of the Act means the purchase of student loan-backed securities and affairs related thereto.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Jun. 7, 2011]

Article 25 Deleted. <Jun. 7, 2011>

Article 26 (Limit of guarantees) The aggregate limit of credit guarantees under Article 28(1) of the Act shall be 20 times the sum of the funds referred to in Article 26(1)1 of the Act and retained earnings carried forward. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>

Article 27 (Guarantee fees) (1) The rate of guarantee fees for calculating guarantee fees under Article 31(1) of the Act shall be the sum of a fixed rate and a period-based additional rate. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>
  (2) The fixed rate under paragraph (1) shall be 1/100 of the amount guaranteed, and the period-based additional rate under that paragraph shall be determined by the Minister of Education, taking into consideration the creditworthiness of the guaranteed person and the period of guarantee use, within a range not exceeding 4/100 of the amount guaranteed. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013>
  (3) The rate of additional guarantee fees for calculating additional guarantee fees under Article 31(2) of the Act shall be 25/10,000 per annum. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>
  (4) The guarantee fees under paragraph (1) shall be collected in a lump sum for the entire period from the date on which the principal obligation is performed and the guarantee liability arises until the expiration of the guarantee period.
  (5) Where all or part of a guarantee is terminated during the guarantee period, the guarantee fees corresponding to the period from the date of termination until the expiration of the guarantee period shall be refunded.

Article 28 (Performance of guaranteed obligations) "Where a ground for default prescribed by Presidential Decree arises" in Article 32(1) of the Act means where a debtor who has obtained a loan through a credit guarantee fails to perform his or her obligation to the creditor within the prescribed period (including where the debtor has forfeited the benefit of time), and a period publicly notified by the Minister of Education, taking into consideration economic conditions and other relevant circumstances, has elapsed, which period shall be not less than 3 months and not more than 18 months. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011; Mar. 23, 2013>

Article 29 (Scope of subordinate obligations) "Subordinate obligations prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 32(2) of the Act means any of the following obligations: <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011>
  1. Interest calculated at the interest rate applicable to the agreed term of the principal obligation, for the period from the date on which the repayment period of the obligation of a person guaranteed under Article 32(1) of the Act expires (including where the person has forfeited the benefit of time) until the Foundation performs the guaranteed obligation;
  2. Expenses incurred by the creditor for the collection of the claim.

Article 30 (Deferral of exercise of indemnity claims) "Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 33(2)3 of the Act means any of the following cases: <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011; Sep. 30, 2014; Jun. 30, 2020>
  1. Where the principal debtor has been recognized as eligible to receive job-seeking benefits pursuant to Article 43 of the Employment Insurance Act;
  2. Where the principal debtor serves in any of the following capacities pursuant to the Military Service Act:
  a. An active-duty soldier under Article 16 of the Military Service Act;
  b. A member of the full-time reserve service under Article 21 of the Military Service Act;
  c. A social service agent under Article 26 of the Military Service Act;
  d. An alternative service worker under Article 17 of the Act on the Assignment and Service of Alternative Military Service Personnel;
  3. Where the Foundation otherwise deems it necessary to defer the exercise of an indemnity claim against the principal debtor.

Article 30-2 (Exemption from indemnity obligations) (1) To obtain an exemption from all or part of an indemnity obligation pursuant to Article 34(1) of the Act, any of the following persons shall apply to the Foundation for exemption from all or part of the indemnity obligation by submitting an application for exemption from an indemnity obligation together with relevant documents: <Amended on Apr. 14, 2020>
  1. The person concerned or his or her agent;
  2. The spouse, a lineal blood relative, or a collateral blood relative within the 4th degree of kinship.
  (2) The relevant documents referred to in the part other than the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) shall be as follows: <Amended on Apr. 14, 2020>
  1. A family relations certificate under Article 15(1)1 of the Act on the Registration of Family Relationships (attached only where the application is filed by a person falling under paragraph (2)2);
  1-2. A power of attorney or other document proving the agency relationship (attached only where an agent files the application under paragraph (1)1);
  2. A death certificate or other document proving death (attached only where the person concerned is deceased);
  3. A disability registration card under Article 32(1) of the Welfare of Persons with Disabilities Act, a document confirming that the person is a beneficiary under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Act on the Guarantee of Income for Persons with Disabilities, or any other document confirming the degree of the person's physical or mental disability (attached only in cases other than the death of the person concerned);
  4. Where an heir of the person concerned has obtained qualified acceptance of inheritance, a document proving that fact (attached only where the person concerned is deceased).
  (3) Upon receiving an application for exemption under paragraph (1), the Foundation may exempt repayment of all or part of the indemnity obligation in consideration of the degree of physical or mental disability, income, assets, and other relevant circumstances of the principal debtor. In such cases, detailed matters concerning the standards for exemption from repayment shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Education. <Added on Apr. 14, 2020>
  [This Article Added on Jun. 7, 2011]

Article 31 (Use of the scholarship account) (1) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 38(1)5 of the Act means expenses for promoting the project under Article 38(1)1 of the Act. <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011; Sep. 30, 2014>
  (2) Deleted. <Sep. 30, 2014>
  (3) Deleted. <Sep. 30, 2014>
  [Title Amended on Jun. 7, 2011]

Article 32 Deleted. <Jun. 7, 2011>

Article 33 Deleted. <Jun. 7, 2011>

CHAPTER IV SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS

Article 33-2 (Term of office) (1) The term of office of commissioned members of the Deliberative Committee shall be 2 years.
  (2) Where a commissioned member vacates office, the term of office of the successor shall be the remainder of the predecessor's term.
  (3) Where a member of the Deliberative Committee under Article 49-3(5)1 through 8 of the Act falls under any of the following subparagraphs, the Minister of Education may remove or dismiss such member from office: <Added on Dec. 31, 2015>
  1. Where the member becomes unable to perform his or her duties due to a mental or physical disability;
  2. Where there is a fact of misconduct related to his or her duties;
  3. Where the member is deemed unsuitable as a member due to neglect of duty, loss of dignity, or other reasons;
  4. Where the member expresses his or her intention that it is difficult to perform his or her duties.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 7, 2011]
  [Title Amended on Dec. 31, 2015]

Article 33-3 (Operation of the Deliberative Committee) (1) The chairperson of the Deliberative Committee shall convene meetings of the Deliberative Committee and preside over them.
  (2) Where the chairperson of the Deliberative Committee is unable to perform his or her duties due to unavoidable circumstances, a member designated in advance by the chairperson shall act on his or her behalf.
  (3) Meetings of the Deliberative Committee shall be opened with the attendance of a majority of the incumbent members, and resolutions shall be adopted by the affirmative vote of a majority of the members present.
  (4) Minutes shall be prepared regarding the proceedings of the Deliberative Committee.
  (5) Allowances and travel expenses may be paid, within budgetary limits, to members attending meetings of the Deliberative Committee; provided, this shall not apply where a member who is a public official attends a meeting in direct connection with his or her official duties.
  (6) Matters necessary for the operation of the Deliberative Committee, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (5), shall be determined by the Chairperson of the Deliberative Committee following a resolution of the Deliberative Committee.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 7, 2011]

Article 33-4 (Priority tuition fee aid) (1) "University students prescribed by Presidential Decree, including recipients of livelihood benefits or medical benefits under Article 7(1)1 or 3 of the National Basic Living Security Act and all children of families raising 3 or more children through childbirth or adoption" in Article 49-4(1) of the Act means a person falling under any of the following subparagraphs who also meets the criteria determined by the Foundation in consideration of the household income amount of the university student (meaning the sum of income and the amount obtained by converting assets into income in accordance with standards determined by the Foundation; hereinafter the same shall apply) and the university student's academic performance: <Amended on Aug. 29, 2016; Jun. 22, 2021>
  1. A recipient of livelihood benefits or medical benefits under Article 7(1)1 or 3 of the National Basic Living Security Act;
  2. Any child of a family raising 3 or more children through childbirth or adoption;
  3. Any other person whom the Foundation deems necessary to receive priority tuition fee aid in order to prevent the inability to receive higher education due to economic circumstances.
  (2) The scope of income included in the income amount under paragraph (1) shall be as follows: <Amended on Jun. 21, 2016; Sep. 18, 2018; Jun. 9, 2020>
  1. Earned income: Income acquired by supply of labor; provided, the earned income which is non-taxable under the Income Tax Act shall be excluded herefrom, but the following salaries shall be included in the earned income:
  a. Non-taxable salaries prescribed in subparagraph 3q of Article 12 of the Income Tax Act;
  b. Non-taxable salaries prescribed in Article 16(1)1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Income Tax Act;
  2. Business income:
  a. Agricultural income: Income from seeds cultivation business, fruit growing and horticultural business, sericultural business, plant nursery business, special crops-producing business, cattle feeding business, breeding stock business, or hatchery business, and business incidental thereto;
  b. Forestry income: Income from forest management business, forest products business, or wild bird and animal breeding business, and business incidental thereto;
  c. Income from fishery: Income from fishery and business incidental thereto;
  d. Other business income: Income from wholesale business, retail business, manufacturing business, or any other business;
  3. Property income:
  a. Lease income: Income accruing from the lease of real estate, movable assets, rights, or any other property;
  b. Interest income: Income exceeding the amount determined by the Foundation, generated from interest, dividends, or discounts on deposits, stocks, or bonds;
  c. Pension income: Pensions arising under Article 20-3(1)2 of the Income Tax Act and income arising under Article 4(1)1b of the Insurance Business Act (excluding housing pensions, farmland pensions, and other pensions determined by the Foundation);
  4. Public transfer income: Various types of allowances, pensions, benefits or other money, and valuables paid or received regularly under statutes, such as the National Pension Act, the Public Officials Pension Act, the Public Officials' Accident Compensation Act, the Military Pension Act, the Military Accident Compensation Act, the Special Post Offices Act, the Pension for Private School Teachers and Staff Act, the Employment Insurance Act, the Industrial Accident Compensation Insurance Act, the Act on the Honorable Treatment of Persons of Distinguished Service to Independence, the Act on the Honorable Treatment of and Support for Persons of Distinguished Service to the State, the Act on Support for Persons Eligible for Veteran's Compensation, the Act on Assistance to Patients Suffering from Actual or Potential Aftereffects of Defoliants and Establishment of Related Organizations, the Compulsory Motor Vehicle Liability Security Act, and the Act on Honorable Treatment of War Veterans and Establishment of Related Associations; provided, the following money and valuables shall be excluded herefrom (excluding allowances, pensions, and benefits determined by the Foundation, such as benefits for persons of distinguished service to the State):
  (3) The scope of assets included in the income amount under paragraph (1) shall be as follows:
  1. General property:
  a. Land, buildings, housing, aircraft, and ships referred to in subparagraphs 1 through 5 of Article 104 of the Local Tax Act;
  b. Lease deposits (including lease deposits) for houses, commercial buildings, and other properties determined by the Foundation;
  c. Movable property, including livestock and seedlings, valued at KRW 1 million or more (excluding rehabilitation assistive devices for persons with disabilities and other movable property determined by the Foundation), and standing timber under subparagraph 11 of Article 6 of the Local Tax Act;
  d. Fishery rights under subparagraph 13 of Article 6 of the Local Tax Act;
  e. Membership rights under subparagraphs 14 through 18 of Article 6 of the Local Tax Act;
  f. Membership rights to occupancy in a housing association under Article 89(2) of the Income Tax Act;
  g. Rights to acquire a building and the land appurtenant thereto upon completion of the building (excluding membership rights to occupancy under subparagraph 1e);
  2. Financial property:
  a. Financial assets referred to in subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act on Real Name Financial Transactions and Confidentiality;
  b. Various types of insurances referred to in Article 4(1) of the Insurance Business Act;
  3. Motor vehicles under Article 124 of the Local Tax Act (excluding motor vehicles owned by persons with disabilities and other motor vehicles determined by the Foundation).
  (4) The value of assets under the subparagraphs of paragraph (3) shall be calculated, as of the date on which the relevant institution or organization investigates the relevant information at the request of the Minister of Education or the Foundation pursuant to Article 50-2 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "date of investigation"), in accordance with the following methods; provided, where it is difficult to calculate the value of an asset, the value shall be calculated in accordance with standards determined by the Foundation in consideration of the type of asset and market transaction conditions: <Amended on Feb. 9, 2018; Dec. 31, 2021>
  1. Paragraph (3)1a: A value determined by the Foundation taking into account the standard market value under Article 4 of the Local Tax Act;
  2. Paragraph (3)1b: The deposit or lease deposit stated in the lease agreement;
  3. Paragraph (3)1c: For movable property, the market value as of the date of investigation; for standing timber, the standard market value under Article 4(1)5 of the Enforcement Decree of the Local Tax Act;
  4. Paragraph (3)1d: The standard market value under Article 4(1)8 of the Enforcement Decree of the Local Tax Act;
  5. Paragraph (3)1e: The standard market value under Article 4(1)9 of the Enforcement Decree of the Local Tax Act;
  6. Paragraph (3)1f: The amount determined as follows:
  a. Where liquidation money is paid: The total amount of price determined by the management and disposal plans referred to in Article 74 of the Act on the Improvement of Urban Areas and Residential Environments (hereinafter referred to as "estimated value of the existing building") and liquidation money paid;
  b. Where liquidation money is received: An amount calculated by deducting the amount of liquidation money from the estimated value of the existing building;
  7. Paragraph (3)1g: The amount paid in as of the date of investigation;
  8. Paragraph (3)2: The value of each financial asset calculated in accordance with the standards under Article 33-6(1) and (3);
  9. Paragraph (3)3: A value determined by the Foundation taking into consideration the type of vehicle, seating capacity, maximum load capacity, manufacturer's price by year of manufacture (or import price in the case of imported vehicles), transaction price, and other relevant factors.
  (5) "Low-income students and others prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 49-4(2) of the Act means a university student or high school student who falls under any of the subparagraphs of Article 21-2(2) (excluding subparagraph 6). <Added on Jun. 22, 2021>
  (6) The information under Article 49-4(2) of the Act shall include the following matters: <Added on Jun. 22, 2021>
  1. Types and amounts of tuition fee aid;
  2. Eligibility for tuition fee aid;
  3. Methods and procedures for applying for tuition fee aid;
  4. Other matters deemed necessary by the Minister of Education.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Sep. 30, 2014]

Article 33-5 (Method of applying for tuition fee aid) (1) A person intending to receive tuition fee aid pursuant to Article 50(1) of the Act (including tuition fee aid under Article 16(1)5 of the Act) shall submit an application by electronic means through the information and communications network designated by the Minister of Education and the Foundation.
  (2) A person intending to receive tuition fee aid pursuant to paragraph (1), and his or her parents or spouse, shall submit to the Minister of Education or the Foundation a written consent to the provision of the data or information referred to in the subparagraphs of Article 50(2) of the Act and the subparagraphs of Article 33-7(1), either directly or through the information and communications network; provided, such written consent may be submitted by mail or facsimile in any of the following cases:
  1. Where direct submission or submission through the information and communications network is impossible due to residence abroad or any similar reason;
  2. Where the parent or spouse of the person intending to receive tuition fee aid is a foreign national and direct submission or submission through the information and communications network is impossible;
  3. Where the Minister of Education and the Foundation otherwise deem direct submission or submission through the information and communications network impracticable.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 30, 2014]

Article 33-6 (Scope of financial information) (1) "Average balance of deposits and other data or information prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 50(2)12 of the Act means the following data or information:
  1. Demand deposits, such as an ordinary deposit, a saving deposit and a preferential savings deposit: Average balance within the preceding 3 months;
  2. Savings deposits, such as a term deposit, installment savings deposit, a term saving: Balance of deposit or total amount of deposit;
  3. Stocks, beneficiary certificates, investment money, investment shares, or real estate (annuity) trusts: The latest market value; in such cases, Article 54(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Inheritance Tax and Gift Tax Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the evaluation of the value of unlisted stocks;
  4. Bonds, promissory notes, checks, debt certificates, certificates of preemptive right to new stocks, or transferable deposit certificates: The face value;
  5. Annuity savings: The amount paid periodically or the final balance.
  (2) "Amount of liabilities and other data or information prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 50(2)13 of the Act means the following data or information:
  1. Current status of loans and details of arrears;
  2. Unsettled credit card balance.
  (3) "Insurance premiums and other insurance-related data or information prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 50(2)14 of the Act means the following data or information:
  1. Insurance policies: Amount to be refunded if the insurance contracts are canceled, or insurance money paid within the latest 1 year;
  2. Annuity insurances: Amount to be refunded if the insurance contracts are canceled, or periodically paid amount of money.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 30, 2014]

Article 33-7 (Request for and updating of data) (1) The specific scope of data that the Minister of Education and the Foundation may request pursuant to Article 50-2(1)15 of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on Sep. 18, 2018; Jun. 22, 2021>
  1. Data concerning income, assets, work capacity, and employment status: The following data:
  a. Data concerning employment insurance from the Korea Employment Information Service under the Framework Act on Employment Policy;
  b. Data concerning industrial accident compensation insurance from the Korea Workers' Compensation and Welfare Service under the Industrial Accident Compensation Insurance Act;
  c. Data concerning the national pension from the National Pension Service under the National Pension Act;
  d. Data concerning the government employees pension and compensation for public officials' accidents from the Government Employees Pension Service under the Government Employees Pension Act;
  e. Data concerning the military pension from the Minister of National Defense;
  f. Data concerning the private school teachers and staff pension from the Teachers' Pension Service under the Pension for Private School Teachers and Staff Act;
  g. Data concerning the post office pension from the Specific Post Office Pension Service Agency under the Special Post Offices Act;
  h. Data concerning business registration information from the Commissioner of the National Tax Service;
  i. Data concerning lawsuits for revocation and restoration of fraudulent acts under Article 25 of the National Tax Collection Act, Article 39 of the Local Tax Collection Act, Article 18 of the Act on the Collection of Local Administrative Sanctions and Charges, or Article 406 of the Civil Act, from the Commissioner of the National Tax Service, the head of a local government, or the Minister of the National Court Administration;
  2. Data concerning protection and services provided under social security-related statutes: Data concerning protection and services provided under the National Health Insurance Act, Act on Welfare of Persons with Disabilities, Disability Pension Act, Single-Parent Family Support Act, Act on the Honorable Treatment of and Support for Persons of Distinguished Service to the State, and other relevant statutes;
  3. Other data: Data concerning immigration and correctional administration from the Minister of Justice, and data necessary for identifying welfare needs and providing services from the Minister of Health and Welfare.
  (2) The Minister of Education and the Foundation shall update the data under Article 50-2(1) of the Act on a regular basis; provided, this shall not apply where there is no need to update such data due to the graduation, leave of absence, withdrawal, or other similar circumstance of a person eligible for tuition fee aid.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 30, 2014]

Article 34 Deleted. <Sep. 30, 2014>

Article 35 (Scope of nonprofit scholarship corporations) "Nonprofit scholarship corporations prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 50-2(2) of the Act means corporations satisfying all of the following requirements: <Amended on Sep. 30, 2014; Aug. 29, 2016>
  1. A public-interest corporation under Article 2 of the Act on the Establishment and Operation of Public-Interest Corporations that carries out projects relating to tuition fee aid or scholarships for university students;
  2. A corporation whose aggregate basic funds (meaning the amount obtained by subtracting total liabilities from total assets) belonging to the public-interest project account and the profit-making project account under Article 23(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on the Establishment and Operation of Public-Interest Corporations amount to at least KRW 1 billion as of the settlement of accounts for the immediately preceding year.

Article 35-2 (Request for and provision of financial information) (1) Where the Minister of Education or the Foundation requests financial information, credit information, or insurance information under Article 50(2)12 through 14 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "financial information, etc.") concerning a person who has applied for tuition fee aid pursuant to Article 50 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "tuition fee aid applicant") and his or her parents or spouse from the head of a financial institution, etc. (meaning financial companies, etc. under Article 2(1) of the Act on Real Name Financial Transactions and Confidentiality and comprehensive credit information concentration agencies under Article 25(2)1 of the Credit Information Use and Protection Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) pursuant to Article 50-3 of the Act, such request shall be made by a document (including a document converted into electronic form; hereinafter the same shall apply) stating the following matters: <Amended on Aug. 4, 2020>
  1. The names and resident registration numbers of the tuition fee aid applicant and his or her parents or spouse (where the parent or spouse of the tuition fee aid applicant is a foreign national, the alien registration number or passport number);
  2. The scope of the financial information, etc. requested, the reference date for inquiry, and the inquiry period.
  (2) Where the head of a financial institution, etc. that has received a request under paragraph (1) provides the relevant financial information, etc. to the Minister of Education or the Foundation, such information shall be provided by a document stating the following matters:
  1. The names and resident registration numbers of the tuition fee aid applicant and his or her parents or spouse (where the parent or spouse of the tuition fee aid applicant is a foreign national, the alien registration number or passport number);
  2. Name of the financial company, etc., that provides the financial information, etc.;
  3. Names of financial instruments on which information is provided and the account numbers thereof;
  4. Details of financial information, etc.
  (3) The Minister of Education or the Foundation may request the head of the relevant financial institution, etc. to provide the financial information, etc. under paragraph (1) through the information and communications network of an association, federation, or central association to which the financial institution, etc. belongs.
  [This Article Added on Sep. 30, 2014]
  [Previous Article 35-2 moved to Article 35-3 <Sep. 30, 2014>]

Article 35-3 (Data requested for preventing duplicate support) The types and contents of data that the Minister of Education and the Foundation may request pursuant to Article 50-5(2) of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013; Sep. 30, 2014>
  1. The resident registration number (in the case of a foreign national, the alien registration number or passport number) and personal information of a debtor (including the spouse, where the debtor is married) or a person who has received a scholarship, and of his or her parents;
  2. Data on the current status of educational expense support projects, including the amount of educational expenses (including scholarships; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply) provided as a loan or subsidized, loan balance, arrearages, etc.;
  3. Data concerning the terms and conditions of educational expense support projects, including the timing of providing educational expense loan, term of the loan, loan interest rate, etc.;
  4. Other data deemed necessary by the Minister of Education and the Foundation for verifying other tuition fee aid projects.
  [This Article Added on Jun. 7, 2011]
  [Moved from Article 35-2 <Sep. 30, 2014>]

Article 35-4 (Exemption from the obligation to submit data) (1) Pursuant to the proviso to Article 50-5(2) of the Act, the Minister of Education may exempt an institution from all or part of the obligation to submit data concerning the status of tuition fee aid where any of the following grounds exists:
  1. Where an institution falling under Article 50-5(2)1 through 3 or 6 of the Act provides tuition fee aid for the purpose of compensating losses;
  2. Where data on the current status of educational expense support of the relevant institution is related to an ongoing trial or criminal investigation;
  3. Where there is a concern that disclosing data on the current status of educational expense support of the relevant institution may hinder the protection of the life, health and property of the public;
  4. Where information on the current status of educational expense support of the relevant institution is included in the data already submitted or registered by another institution.
  (2) Any institution that wishes to be wholly or partially exempted from the obligation to submit data on the current status of educational expense support pursuant to the proviso of Article 50-5(2) of the Act shall apply for exemption from obligation of data submission to the Minister of Education within 15 days from the date of receipt of a request prescribed in the main clause of that paragraph. In such cases, a written application determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Education shall be submitted to the Minister of Education, together with documents and materials describing the grounds for such application in detail.
  (3) The Minister of Education shall, within 30 days from the date of receipt of the application referred to in paragraph (2), determine whether to grant exemption from obligation of data submission and the scope of such exemption, and immediately notify the relevant institution of the outcomes of such determination.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 29, 2016]

Article 35-5 (Scope of public-interest corporations) (1) "Corporations of a scale prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 50-5(2)4 of the Act means corporations whose aggregate basic funds (meaning the amount obtained by subtracting total liabilities from total assets) belonging to the public-interest project account and the profit-making project account under Article 23(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on the Establishment and Operation of Public Interest Corporations amount to at least KRW 1 billion as of the settlement of accounts for the immediately preceding year.
  (2) "Institutions or organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 50-5(2)8 of the Act means invested or contributed institutions designated and publicly notified pursuant to Article 5 of the Act on the Operation of Local Government-Invested or -Funded Institutions.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 29, 2016]

Article 35-6 (Persons obligated to return excess student aid and excess amount of student aid) (1) The persons from whom the Minister of Education and the Foundation may recover an excess amount of student aid pursuant to Article 50-5(7) of the Act shall be university students, graduate students, or parents (hereinafter referred to as "persons obligated to return") whose aggregate amount of the following funds received for tuition and other educational expenses required for a semester (hereinafter referred to as "student aid received") exceeds the total amount of student aid actually required for that semester (hereinafter referred to as "required student aid"):
  1. The aggregate amount of tuition fee aid provided without charge and amounts borrowed for student aid purposes from institutions referred to in Article 50-5(2)1 through 8 of the Act;
  2. The aggregate amount of tuition fee aid provided without charge and amounts borrowed for student aid purposes from the Minister of Education and the Foundation under the Act or the Special Act on Income Contingent Loan.
  (2) Notwithstanding paragraph (1), the Minister of Education may exclude from the category of persons obligated to return any university student, graduate student, or parent where he or she deems it inappropriate to require the return of the excess amount of student aid pursuant to Article 50-5(7) of the Act, taking into consideration the reasons for the excess student aid, the amount of the excess student aid, the income level, and other relevant factors.
  (3) The excess amount of student aid recoverable by the Minister of Education and the Foundation pursuant to Article 50-5(7) of the Act shall be the amount obtained by subtracting the required student aid from the student aid received; provided, where such amount exceeds the amount referred to in paragraph (1)2, the recoverable amount shall be limited to the amount referred to in paragraph (1)2.
  (4) In order to recover an excess amount of student aid pursuant to Article 50-5(7) of the Act, the Minister of Education and the Foundation shall notify the person obligated to return, in writing, of the requirement to return the excess amount of student aid, specifying the grounds for return, method of return, amount subject to return, deadline for return, and procedures for handling the returned amount. In such cases, the deadline for return shall be set at least 30 days after the date of notification.
  (5) The Minister of Education and the Foundation shall handle amounts returned pursuant to Article 50-5(7) of the Act in accordance with the following subparagraphs. In such cases, where any amount remains after processing under subparagraph 1, such remaining amount shall be processed in accordance with subparagraph 2:
  1. Where the person obligated to return has student loans under the Act or the Framework Act on Repayment of Student Loans after Employment that have not been fully repaid: The returned amount shall be treated as repayment by the person obligated to return of the principal, interest, and related obligations of the student loan to the extent of the returned amount; in such cases, Articles 477 through 479 of the Civil Act shall apply mutatis mutandis to the appropriation of such repayment;
  2. Where the person obligated to return has no outstanding student loans under the Act or the Framework Act on Repayment of Student Loans after Employment: The returned amount shall be treated as revenue of the account or fund serving as the financial source of tuition fee aid provided without charge by the Minister of Education or the Foundation, in accordance with the standards and order determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Education.
  [This Article Added on Aug. 29, 2016]

Article 36 (Entrustment of duties) (1) Pursuant to Article 51(2) of the Act, the Foundation may entrust the following affairs to financial companies, etc.: <Amended on Jun. 7, 2011; Sep. 30, 2014>
  1. Management of surplus funds under Article 23-2 of the Act;
  2. Exercise of claims arising from general repayment student loans under Article 24-10 of the Act;
  3. Exercise of indemnity claims under Article 33 of the Act;
  4. Subscription for bonds under Article 8.
  (2) The Foundation may entrust the affairs referred to in paragraph (1)2 and 3 to the Korea Asset Management Corporation established under the Act on the Establishment of Korea Asset Management Corporation, or to a debt collection company under subparagraph 10-2 of Article 2 of the Credit Information Use and Protection Act. <Amended on Oct. 1, 2009; Jun. 7, 2011; Mar. 24, 2014; Aug. 4, 2020; Jun. 22, 2021>

Article 36-2 (Processing of sensitive information and unique identifying information) The Minister of Education (including a person entrusted with the duties of the Minister of Education pursuant to Article 51 of the Act) and the Foundation may, where unavoidable for carrying out any of the following affairs, process data containing criminal records data under subparagraph 2 of Article 18 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act and resident registration numbers, passport numbers, or alien registration numbers under subparagraph 1, 2, or 4 of Article 19 of that Decree:
  1. Affairs concerning applications for tuition fee aid under Article 50 of the Act;
  2. Affairs concerning requests for the submission of data under Article 50-2(1) of the Act;
  3. Affairs concerning the linked use of information systems under Article 50-2(5) of the Act;
  4. Affairs concerning requests for the provision of financial information, etc. under Article 50-3(1) of the Act;
  5. Affairs concerning requests for data and questioning under Article 50-4 of the Act;
  6. Affairs concerning the prevention of duplicate support under Article 50-5 of the Act.
  [This Article Wholly Amended on Sep. 30, 2014]

Article 37 (Courtesy to private contributors) Where the Foundation provides tuition fee aid without charge in the name of a contributor or donor pursuant to Article 53(1) of the Act, it shall issue to the university student receiving such tuition fee aid a certificate of tuition fee aid bearing the name of the contributor or donor, and shall publicly announce the following matters through a nationwide daily newspaper, its website, or any other effective means: <Amended on Nov. 24, 2020>
  1. The name of the contributor or donor and the scale of tuition fee aid provided without charge;
  2. The eligible recipients or scope of university students receiving tuition fee aid without charge;
  3. Other matters that the Foundation deems necessary to announce.

